-- [ENGINE-FRESHNESS] deck_data_version — the committed-write version stamp.
--
-- ONE row (id = 1), bumped by the AFTER triggers below on the two tables the
-- render engine's derived caches are built from (cards + card_references —
-- the entire read surface of the fetch/rule/permission caches). The render
-- path reads the stamp ONCE per request (one PK point SELECT,
-- src/card/render.ts DECK_DATA_VERSION_STATEMENT) and clears its derived
-- caches when the value moved, so a warm runtime serves the committed state
-- with no staleness window and a revoked read can no longer be served from a
-- cached verdict (delivery contract:79 — "No stale-success response or
-- post-revocation data leak is acceptable").
--
-- The bump lives in TRIGGERS, not in an application write API, because a
-- committed write is authoritative regardless of which stream or process
-- issued it: the storage layer is the one place every committed write
-- crosses, including raw SQL, the future write path, and another runtime's
-- connection. Write cost: one extra single-row UPDATE per mutation (point
-- write on a 1-row b-tree). Read cost: one PK point SELECT per request —
-- the render contract's warm-render cost is ONE statement, not zero.
--
-- Already-provisioned databases get this block from
-- deploy/migrations/0003-deck-data-version-stamp.sql (IF NOT EXISTS /
-- INSERT OR IGNORE, order-safe against the regenerated 0001). A database
-- WITHOUT the stamp table fails the render-path read LOUDLY by design: a
-- silently skipped check would be a silent staleness window.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
